  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM EDT Sun Oct 18 2026\n\nThru Tonight...Windy conditions this afternoon will decrease with\nsunset over inland areas but remain breezy/gusty along the coast\nout of the Northeast. A Wind Advisory remains in effect for\ncoastal Volusia/Brevard/Indian River counties thru 8 PM and is not\nlikely to be extended. Despite drier air, persistent NE flow is \npicking up some Atlc moisture which will generate isolated to \nscattered showers over the Atlc. These fast moving showers will \ncross portions of the coast and push well inland so a 20-30% PoP\nis drawn areawide. Any showers will be brief but there may be a\ncouple/few rounds in some locations. Min temps will remain very \nmild along the coast in the low to mid 70s, cooling into the mid \nto upper 60s over the interior. \n\nMon...NE winds persist; remaining windy along the coast with\nspeeds 15-25 mph gusting 30-35 mph so do not anticipate another \nWind Advisory. Breezy/gusty over the interior with gusts up to 30\nmph. This persistent fresh northeast flow will continue to produce\na number of beach and marine hazards...including rough surf, \nlarge breaking waves, and a high risk of life-threatening rip \ncurrents at area beaches. Entering the dangerous surf is highly \ndiscouraged! Long period NE swells will also contribute to \nmoderate beach erosion following multiple high tide cycles. A \nHigh Risk for rip currents exists for area beaches and will likely\ncontinue through much of this week. A High Surf Advisory \nremains in effect through early this week as seas continue to \nbuild. \n\nOnshore, fast moving showers will continue with 20-30% coverage.\nNo lightning is forecast as instability remains very low. Max\ntemps look very similar to today...upper 70s Lake/Volusia and\nlow 80s metro Orlando/Brevard and mid 80s Okeechobee/Treasure\ncoast.\n\nTue-Sat...A persistent weather pattern is forecast into midweek. \nThe low pressure off the mid Atlc will finally lift NE away from\nthe US east coast by late Thu. This will allow the pressure\ngradient to relax some resulting in decreased low level winds. But\nbreezy conds of 15-20 mph can be expected along the coast each \nday. Models indicate mostly dry conditions through this time, but\ncannot rule out isolated light showers pushing onshore at times. \nThe strong ridge remains cemented across the eastern United \nStates, as weak low/disturbance may take form near the SE Bahamas\nalong an old frontal boundary. The pressure gradient between \nthese two features may once again tighten into the weekend, with \nonshore winds gradually strengthening once again, producing breezy\nto windy conditions and a threat for additional beach erosion.\n\nTemperatures are forecast to remain below normal through the period, \nwith highs in the upper 70s/low 80s and overnight lows mostly in the \n60s, except upper 50s NW of I-4.",
